## What I learned
- The cascade is a 3-round tournament:
  1. Direct targeting vs inherited → directly targeted rules ALWAYS win
     (inherited styles don't even reach round 2)
  2. Specificity: inline > ID > class > type — compared by RANK first,
     like Olympic medals: 1 class beats 100 types, 1 ID beats 100 classes
  3. Rule order: ONLY the tie-breaker of tie-breakers — last rule wins
- My old belief "latest wins" wasn't wrong, it was just the LAST question
  the judge asks, not the first
- DevTools: F12 → Elements panel shows the DOM tree
  (the browser's LIVE map of the page, not my source file)
- A strikethrough in the Styles panel = a rule that LOST the tournament —
  not invalid, just overridden. Best debugging clue in CSS
- element.style box = inline style = top specificity rank,
  which is why edits there always show up instantly
- English reading rule: always look up TECH terms; skip general words
  if the sentence still makes sense; look one up after its 3rd appearance

